WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.769 --> 00:00:07.254
First of all welcome back.

00:00:07.254 --> 00:00:11.643
It's so exciting to see so
many familiar faces.

00:00:11.643 --> 00:00:14.823
Many of you don't look different
at all in the last five years,

00:00:14.823 --> 00:00:16.031
so you look fantastic.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.180
Let me just say that.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.190
I also wanted to give you kind of
a road map of what we're going to do.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.370
I'm going to talk a little bit about
why I'm happy to be here right now.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:30.540
Friday night with you
guys without alcohol,

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.280
but I know it's outside and luring you.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.620
So, I'm going to try and
make sure that this is sort of spiced up.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.470
Then I'm going to talk a little bit
about the power of story in two regards.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:46.140
The first is as an asset,
strategically for business.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.309
Raise your hands if you've
founded your own company or

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:54.336
are working sort of at your own company
in some way, shape, or form, so

00:00:54.336 --> 00:00:57.108
that's like I don't
know 25% of the people.

00:00:57.108 --> 00:01:02.468
And how many of you have
been at a company that you

00:01:02.468 --> 00:01:07.696
started with right when
you finished the GSV?

00:01:07.696 --> 00:01:09.763
Raise your hands.

00:01:09.763 --> 00:01:12.550
That's about 20%.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.300
So, I'm going to be
asking both of you groups

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.010
of people a set of questions about sort of
thinking about story as a strategic asset.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:25.360
Then I'm going to turn to this idea of
story as life, this idea that story may

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:31.450
be better than anything else in our life
both captures meaning and creates meaning.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:36.350
So, and then I'm going to talk a little
bit about what I hope you do on Monday.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:44.730
So first of all, before I dive in,
I wanted to lay out a framework

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:50.220
of some recent research that I've done
with Seth Cumber and Cassie Mogilner,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:54.360
and this is based off of a data
set called We Feel Fine,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.200
which Seth Cumber and
Jonathan Harris pulled together.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:03.240
And the way to read this simple slide is,
which is, by the way,

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:09.800
essentially a summary of
over 10,000 data points over

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.930
three years time over,
across cultures, and it's blog based

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.910
data where Seth and Jonathan scrape
the blogosphere for all mentions of.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:21.110
I feel and I am feeling.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:25.580
And then they can co-vary what people
are feeling, and what they're saying,

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:29.820
doing, feeling, etc, when they're
talking about certain emotions.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:33.210
Because blog based data has
demographics associated with it.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:38.170
You can then start to better understand
sort of the emotional lexicon of

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:39.220
our lives.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.390
So the way to read this slide
is we start out simple,

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:49.320
this is your 11 to 14 year old son,
daughter, nephew, niece, sibling who

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:55.530
when you say how are you you
feeling they say, how is your day?

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:57.490
What did you do today?

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.230
Exactly.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:01.100
You know them.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.750
You have them in your life.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.790
But soon we fill up with angst, so

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.130
this is the 15 to 18 year olds
who feel a great deal of angst.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.370
When they do feel happy,
it is equated with excitement.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:17.840
Quite literally happiness and excitement
co-vary in a very significant way.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.970
And it's not that they feel
excited all the time or

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:24.080
angsty all the time it's that the meaning
of happiness, the definition of happiness

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.840
is associated with excitement until
you leave those feelings, and

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.630
then started to feel more unknown,
confined, alone.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:37.920
At 19, 20, 21, I started to feel
not well understood often times.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.210
And then we started to feel that we
could conquer the world at 22 to 26.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:46.990
And now we start to feel that
we actually have a goal.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:48.390
That we know what we're doing.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:54.080
The meaning of happiness starts to equate
with feeling proud or satisfied or

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.840
still excited before gradually
trading ambition for balance.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.810
So at 30, we start to say we're
imbalanced, we forgot to have kids,

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:07.520
I stopped working out, oops, and so

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.840
the feeling of happiness is equated
with feeling aligned again.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.160
Developing appreciation for
our physical bodies.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.000
At 35, they've gone downhill in
a very systematic way, surprisingly.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.980
We wake up and we're like wow,
how did that happen?

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.950
And our children,

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:32.190
an evolving sense of in which we feel
grateful, happy, calm, and then blessed.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.315
And as some of you know with this data,
it is not that you die at 55.

00:04:36.315 --> 00:04:40.440
It's that there is just not
the rich emotional lexicon.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:45.630
And I also actually, this is actually
12 million data points on 10 thousand.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:49.060
So this is 12 million data points
worldwide, so it is a very,

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:54.590
very large data set to help unpack
the emotional lexicon of our lives.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:55.942
So what we've done with this,

00:04:55.942 --> 00:04:58.870
is we've started to say if this
is the story of our lives.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:03.580
And if this is, in fact,
the story of the lives of our customers.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.060
So, even if you've captured strong
value through your customer,

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:12.465
this research would suggest that
the value propositions going to shift in

00:05:12.465 --> 00:05:17.380
pre-systematic ways every 5 or 10 years,
and it's the story of our employees.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:21.900
So, even if again your employee set
is incredibly happy, this research

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:27.090
would suggest that in 5 or 10 years
they're going to be shifting again.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:31.070
So, the way to think about this,
and it's not just across your life,

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:34.960
this is sort of the story of a project or
even a day you get here for

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.350
the alumni weekend, and you have again
this sort of story that's unfolding.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:40.670
I'd be really curious.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:45.050
I invite anyone to actually tell me
at the end of this reunion weekend,

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.740
and just email me, @stanford,
what was the story of the weekend?

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.500
What's the story that you actually
are going to tell your friends,

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.000
family, whatever about this weekend?

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.440
You're not going to say like I went
on Friday and I told Jennifer,

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.790
and then I went on Saturday and
I saw my friends.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:00.900
You're not going to do that.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.130
You're going to tell a story and

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:05.250
that's the way you're going
to remember this experience.

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:06.880
So, you start out with discovery.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.830
You know, as we are at this moment
where you don't really know your goals.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:14.250
You don't really know what's going to
happen and the meaning of happiness,

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:15.559
again, is about excitement.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:21.120
Then you move to pursuit, so you might
know your goals, you want to try and

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.490
talk to a few people or
be able to connect.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:29.010
And there, the feeling is I'm
feeling like I'm winning, or

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.870
I'm conquering the world or
at least I know what I'm doing.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:36.530
And then you move to balance and you say
how can I align my friends, my family,

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:41.160
my work, my health, and then you
move to impact where you wake up and

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.950
you say man I've been so focused or

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:49.390
focused on my family, but
really what is my impact in the world, and

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:54.230
what is the pro social benefit
that I want to be able to create

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:58.630
with a meaning of happiness is much more
associated with feeling significant.

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:01.770
And then you move to savoring
where instead of being more

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.860
forward looking you're
more present oriented,

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:08.620
and the meaning of happiness is associated
with feeling content in the here and now.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:13.640
And how many of you know individuals that,
and again it's not just over your life,

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:18.520
there's some individuals that just
feel very content and savoring even if

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:23.480
they're 18 and conversely there's 55 Five
year olds who are excited all of the time.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:26.430
And in fact, we have research to show that

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:30.960
if we give 18 year olds the task to
breathe deeply, focused on the present.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.290
Like pretend you guys do,
in fact, do it all now.

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:34.830
Breathe deeply.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:36.280
Focus on the present.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:37.715
You guys breathe normal.

00:07:37.715 --> 00:07:43.640
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, keep it up for the whole talk.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:48.091
I, it's incredibly hard to do, but I
can't begin to tell you the physiological

00:07:48.091 --> 00:07:51.966
benefit that it will actually give you,
so I challenge you to do that.

00:07:51.966 --> 00:07:55.007
And then these 18 year olds,
breath normally, and

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:58.330
we come back and
we ask them two very different questions.

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:03.107
You know, what is the meaning of happiness
and you guys define it as Excitement,

00:08:03.107 --> 00:08:05.086
there's one person listening.

00:08:05.086 --> 00:08:08.592
I love you, fantastic.

00:08:08.592 --> 00:08:11.490
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then you guys,

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:15.580
after breathing deeply, focused on
the present and feeling very connected,

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:20.390
actually define happiness as
contentment like a 55 year old would.

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:23.990
So for various, sort of,
with five minutes.

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:27.810
A deep breathing focused on the present
in a more meditative state.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.440
You can actually change your mindset.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.520
In fact, we give you guys choices like
do you want a peppermint refreshing tea.

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:38.660
And you are more likely to pick the
peppermint refreshing tea because often we

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:42.670
make choices based on our definition
of happiness or how to optimize.

00:08:42.670 --> 00:08:46.320
Whereas you are much more likely to
choose a tea that's very peaceful and

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.200
calming like chamomile.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:54.040
So, the point here being that our meaning
of happiness changes over the life course,

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:59.420
over a project, and even within the day
as something as small as five minutes.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:04.420
Now this research was really exciting for
me and I'll start to explain why.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:07.540
The first is that it shows that even

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:12.190
though most people believe that
happiness is either one thing.

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:15.130
Or in fact it's individual,
so all whatever,

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:19.100
300 people here have a unique
definition of happiness.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:23.210
This research suggests that happiness and
its meaning changes in very systematic

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:26.370
ways, all the way from excitement
to essentially contentment.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:30.510
And the second insight is that
what people are valuing and

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:34.930
what they're searching for
is really different.

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:39.180
So what we find is at 18,
we're often valuing

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:43.144
this notion of I just want to feel happy,
and for me that's excitement.

00:09:43.144 --> 00:09:46.826
And at age 30, 40, 50,
you're starting to feel like,

00:09:46.826 --> 00:09:49.610
I just want to feel like I
have meaning in my life.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:51.600
That feeling of fulfillment.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.070
That feeling of joy and contentment.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.520
And so what people are optimizing for, and

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.930
making choices based on,
is a really different utility function.

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:04.910
Now these two things, happiness
versus meaning, are often equated.

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:11.114
In fact, 40% to 50% of the variance in
these two constructs are both positive.

00:10:11.114 --> 00:10:14.300
So it's hard to kind of unpack them.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:17.190
But we've done that, to some degree.

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:20.730
And if anyone's interested,
just Google happiness versus meaning, and

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:22.460
you can dive into the data.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:25.640
But I wanted to summarize
the differences between these

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:27.170
two very positive constructs.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:34.300
Because it has a lot to do with my premise
which is I hope you start leading lives

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:41.082
that are filled with stories to a much
greater degree than you are so far.

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:44.940
So the first difference
between chasing happiness or

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:47.870
living a life based on this idea
of valuing or searching for

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:52.940
happiness, versus meaning is
that happiness feels good.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:55.360
So if you ask people, listen,
how happy are you and

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.040
to what degree do you feel like
you have a meaningful life?

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:01.660
And then people obviously have
different ratings on a 1.7 scale.

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:05.090
You can start to partial out the positive
covariance in those two things.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:07.900
And we can start to peel apart those
individuals who feel very happy,

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.680
but don't feel like they
have a lot of meaning.

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:13.240
And individuals that feel like they have a
lot of meaning, but not necessarily happy.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:17.980
So in that way, we can start to see that
happiness is about really feeling good,

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:21.190
and meaningfulness does too,
but not always.

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:22.760
So there's a lot of low points.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:26.740
Anxiety, fear, etc.,
wrapped up in feeling meaning.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:27.780
There's a lot of ups.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:28.910
There's a lot of downs.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:31.260
It's a roller coaster.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:35.040
So if you think about it from a story
structure perspective, it's not winning,

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:36.310
winning, winning, winning.

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:39.800
It's winning, losing,
winning, trying, winning.

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:43.100
There's an arc to the life of meaning.

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:46.650
The second is that happiness
is more focused on yourself.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:50.575
People that claim to be very happy but
not a lot of meaning

00:11:50.575 --> 00:11:56.120
self-rate themselves as being [LAUGH]
self-focused and more egocentric.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:01.120
The individuals that say that they have
a lot of meaning that maybe are less happy

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:03.330
are very anchored on others.

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:06.030
Giving to others,
feeling connected to others.

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:10.670
And the third and last difference is that
happiness is about how you feel now, and

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:13.850
meaningfulness is about the past,
the present, and the future.

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:15.780
It's much more lasting.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:19.680
So these are three of maybe the more
important differences between meaning,

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:24.320
but it's really hard to ask yourself well,
I'm going to go out and

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:27.340
have a meaningful day, or
where's meaning found.

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:34.946
And the contention would be that meaning
is both captured and created in?

00:12:38.545 --> 00:12:40.750
A second person I love!

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:43.180
Awesome!
Okay, you saw where I was going with this.

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:46.820
So stories, all right, so what is a story?

00:12:47.895 --> 00:12:50.090
Three ways to define it.

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:53.794
It's marked by ups and
downs, highs and lows,

00:12:53.794 --> 00:12:58.340
it's a narrative with a beginning and
a middle and an end.

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:02.493
And again it's not just winning, winning,
winning, there's no tension there,

00:13:02.493 --> 00:13:04.910
there's no evolution,
it's actually an arc.

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:08.590
So if you start to think about
James Bond films, right.

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:09.440
Wow, what happened?

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:12.252
I don't know.
He gets the girl.

00:13:12.252 --> 00:13:15.540
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The arc if very clear and

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:21.610
it's repeated again and again and again,
or Jurassic Park, nothing going on, T-Rex!

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:22.310
Nothing going on.

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:23.160
Big T-Rex!

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:23.750
Nothing going on.

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:25.180
Really really big T-Rex!

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:30.245
So it's not a sophisticated arc but
it's still an arc.

00:13:30.245 --> 00:13:33.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or Casablanca where there's micro or

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:38.990
minis or stories that have arcs
that echo the overall story arc.

00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:45.150
The second thing that pulls apart story
from just a list of facts, or features, or

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:49.490
a to do list,
is that it's typically human centered.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:53.120
There's a protagonist, and
there's characters, and

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:55.460
there's the mix between both of them.

00:13:56.830 --> 00:14:01.130
Many of you know the six word story
written by Ernest Hemingway, who said,

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:04.280
baby shoes for sale, never worn.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:08.880
And we don't know if that's a young
couple who couldn't have a baby,

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:10.810
or the baby didn't make it.

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:14.220
But we do know something about that story.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:17.490
And neurologically, what's happening
when you share a story versus just

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:21.500
say there was a young couple and
they couldn't have a baby.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:26.740
Is that the person listening to the story
is making the inference about the story.

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:30.170
And when people make
the inference about the story

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:35.110
it stays in their mind because it's
being processed by both hemispheres.

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:37.870
Versus when I just share
something to you where

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.430
you don't have to make the inference
because I'm telling it to you.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:43.930
You don't have to process that
information nearly as deeply.

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:45.650
So it's giving people two plus two and

00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:49.090
they are inferring the four,
instead of just giving them four.

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.900
So, how many of you have
been in one of my classes?

00:14:54.526 --> 00:14:55.933
My God, 40%.

00:14:55.933 --> 00:14:59.277
All right, so
you've probably have done this, but

00:14:59.277 --> 00:15:01.788
it's really fun to do it a second time.

00:15:01.788 --> 00:15:03.251
