00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:14.422
The last component of stories is that it
lasts over time from a neurological angle

00:20:14.422 --> 00:20:18.220
goal as well as something that
does last actually over time.

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:22.992
So let me talk a little bit about, let's
see if the volume's on the sciences story.

00:20:26.309 --> 00:20:28.698
&gt;&gt; Once upon a time
information was scarce, so

00:20:28.698 --> 00:20:32.180
we made decisions based
on advise of experts.

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:34.667
Using them as North Stars for instance.

00:20:34.667 --> 00:20:38.607
Now, thanks to the internet, we can
ourselves try and find our own answers to

00:20:38.607 --> 00:20:42.760
questions that plague us, and find
information to make informed decisions.

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:46.400
But instead of finding clear answers,
we often find noise.

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:48.980
We're living in a world where
we have too much information.

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:52.240
And because of that, we're even
more susceptible to great story.

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:54.810
&gt;&gt; It's what helps us
decide what to believe in.

00:20:54.810 --> 00:20:57.750
Stories are important because they're
meaningful, and they are meaningful

00:20:57.750 --> 00:21:02.390
because they're memorable, impactful,
and they personally connect.

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:05.000
First, let's talk about memorability.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:08.030
Eugene O'Kelly was a CEO
of a consulting company.

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:10.670
He started having headaches.

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:14.480
He was referred to a top neurosurgeon in
New York where he was diagnosed with late

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:17.370
stage brain cancer, and
he was given three months to live.

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:19.410
He started talking to his wife.

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:22.350
How does he want to spend
those hundred days?

00:21:22.350 --> 00:21:27.050
He was purposeful about who he spent time
with, and what stories to share with them.

00:21:27.050 --> 00:21:31.270
He also thought hard about what would be
remembered in those last hundred days.

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:36.160
He and his wife would live each day
with a focus on finding perfect moments

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:39.070
where time expands and
when noise is separated from signal.

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:42.030
All that exists is that single moment.

00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:46.504
And he found in those perfect
moments those were the most

00:21:46.504 --> 00:21:49.074
memorable stories that defined his life.

00:21:52.996 --> 00:21:55.826
In one study researchers asked
students to make a one minute

00:21:55.826 --> 00:21:58.730
persuasive pitch to other
members in their class.

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:03.320
And on average a typical student
used 2.5 statistics in their pitch.

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:05.435
Only 1 in 10 told a story.

00:22:05.435 --> 00:22:09.090
10 minutes later, the researcher asked
everyone to pull out a sheet of paper, and

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:11.828
write down every single
idea they remembered.

00:22:11.828 --> 00:22:17.615
Only 5% remembered any statistic,
63% of the students remembered the story.

00:22:17.615 --> 00:22:20.175
Stories are memorable in
a way that statistics aren't.

00:22:21.655 --> 00:22:23.725
Now let me talk about impact.

00:22:23.725 --> 00:22:27.166
Research shows that individuals
are more likely to buy from a person or

00:22:27.166 --> 00:22:30.916
an organization whose story they believe
in and which resonates with them.

00:22:30.916 --> 00:22:33.468
In one study Deb Small at
the University of Pennsylvania and

00:22:33.468 --> 00:22:37.560
her colleagues wanted to determine how to
best raise money for Save the Children.

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:40.720
A charity focused on the well-being
of children worldwide.

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:42.800
We created two versions
of a marketing pamphlet.

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:46.780
The first version features statistics
about the magnitude of problems

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:48.540
facing children in Africa.

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:52.560
The second version also
featured those statistics, but

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:57.200
in addition, provided information about
Rokia, a seven year old girl from Mali.

00:22:57.200 --> 00:22:59.380
Who face the threat of severe hunger.

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:03.300
The subjects were given $5 as compensation
for the study, and before leaving,

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:06.950
the researches asked if they wanted
to donate any money to the cause.

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:09.460
The individuals in the story condition

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:13.110
gave nearly twice as much money as
those in the statistics condition.

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:18.080
We used to think it was our rational
brain that makes decisions.

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:20.690
Now we know it's emotion
driving decisions and

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:23.730
we rationalize the decision afterwards.

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:27.020
Finally, I want to talk
about personal connection.

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:30.590
Research shows that if you present
statistics to an audience, a certain part

00:23:30.590 --> 00:23:33.389
of the brain called the Broca's and
Wernicke's area gets activated.

00:23:34.420 --> 00:23:37.100
So the language processing part
of the brain is activated and

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:38.630
we can understand but not feel.

00:23:39.950 --> 00:23:43.550
When a story is shared,
the audience feels the story.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:45.240
Our whole brain is activated.

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:47.391
The meaning is extracted.

00:23:47.391 --> 00:23:50.649
The meaning of the story comes from
the personal connection the audience feels

00:23:50.649 --> 00:23:52.289
when they're listening to the story.

00:23:52.289 --> 00:23:56.074
And when a story is well-told,
they're able to feel connected,

00:23:56.074 --> 00:23:58.653
not just to the story but
to the storyteller.

00:23:58.653 --> 00:24:01.899
When most of us think of advocating for
ideas, we go to statistics.

00:24:01.899 --> 00:24:06.882
We go to convincing arguments, facts, and
figures, but studies show that if we share

00:24:06.882 --> 00:24:11.387
a story, people are more likely to
remember the message, be persuaded by it,

00:24:11.387 --> 00:24:13.391
feel personally connected to it.

00:24:13.391 --> 00:24:16.268
And when data and
stories are used together,

00:24:16.268 --> 00:24:20.141
audiences are moved both
intellectually and emotionally.

00:24:20.141 --> 00:24:23.816
For lasting effect, you need to
persuade the rational brain, but

00:24:23.816 --> 00:24:26.124
also resonate with the emotional brain.

00:24:26.124 --> 00:24:29.475
Recent behavioral research has
shown why stories are so powerful,

00:24:29.475 --> 00:24:31.305
it's because they are meaningful.

00:24:31.305 --> 00:24:35.204
Those who tell the best stories
will become the best leaders.

00:24:35.204 --> 00:24:40.324
If we consider the simple question,
what is the story or why am I doing this,

00:24:40.324 --> 00:24:44.896
we may finally make very different
choices at how we spend our time.

00:24:44.896 --> 00:24:50.112
Isabel Allende once wrote, silence
before being born, silence after death.

00:24:50.112 --> 00:24:54.509
Life is nothing but noise between
those two unfathomable silences.

00:24:54.509 --> 00:24:59.023
But there is signal and there is noise and
how we reduce the noise?

00:24:59.023 --> 00:25:03.947
Story.

00:25:03.947 --> 00:25:04.964
&gt;&gt; Pretty awesome!

00:25:04.964 --> 00:25:12.321
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
