&gt;&gt; So that single ad, which then
led to Superbowl ads and whatnot,

00:31:25.211 --> 00:31:29.877
was in fact the reason why
Google Chrome started getting adopted.

00:31:29.877 --> 00:31:34.073
And it wasn't just a focus on
Google Chrome, it was really a focus on

00:31:34.073 --> 00:31:38.492
Daniel Lee, this idea of deep empathy
being a way of telling the story,

00:31:38.492 --> 00:31:43.352
where Google Chrome is just enabling
Daniel Lee to be a great dad, and in fact,

00:31:43.352 --> 00:31:45.708
makes us all want to go have kids again,

00:31:45.708 --> 00:31:49.201
because we forgot to do
everything that Daniel Lee did.

00:31:49.201 --> 00:31:54.210
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Damn, could of had more kids.

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:58.889
So Kitchet, I think, has done
a really good job around designing

00:31:58.889 --> 00:32:03.496
around empathy and
really understanding what that user wants.

00:32:03.496 --> 00:32:08.285
I think also MasterClass has
done a really remarkable job by

00:32:08.285 --> 00:32:13.457
David Rogier to say you know,
we want to democratize learning and

00:32:13.457 --> 00:32:20.000
the ability to actually create skills
around story, writing, tennis.

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:21.789
And we're going to do
it in affordable ways.

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:25.810
And I think also Sequoia Creative

00:32:25.810 --> 00:32:28.280
they've done a really good job
from a venture perspective.

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:31.300
A better understanding,
not just their CEO's, but

00:32:31.300 --> 00:32:35.660
the users of the CEO's products and
unlocking that story.

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:39.490
Third and last, a design for purpose.

00:32:39.490 --> 00:32:44.300
This notion that Tom's and
Moby Parker and Body Shop or Ben &amp;

00:32:44.300 --> 00:32:48.450
Jerry's all of these companies that have
In their core what the larger mission, or

00:32:48.450 --> 00:32:53.850
the larger inspired mission is starting,
but slowly I think especially

00:32:53.850 --> 00:32:59.000
here at Stanford to get baked in in
a deeper way in business models.

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:02.970
And you see people like Ben Horowitz
say things like the company's story is

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:04.460
the company's strategy.

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:07.650
The story must explain why you exist,
why we're doing this.

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:09.200
Why does the problem need to be solved?

00:33:09.200 --> 00:33:11.010
Why should you join the company?

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:12.536
Why should you invest in in?

00:33:12.536 --> 00:33:14.533
Why should you buy a product from it?

00:33:14.533 --> 00:33:20.670
So all of these things if the story is
not well told, if it doesn't come across.

00:33:20.670 --> 00:33:23.530
The answers to these questions will be no.

00:33:23.530 --> 00:33:26.860
The thing that's important here is that
people want to be valued members of

00:33:26.860 --> 00:33:32.310
a winning team on an inspired mission and
if you get any one of these words wrong,

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:35.440
that's when you lose momentum,
productivity, performance.

00:33:35.440 --> 00:33:40.810
And maybe the most important word or
two in the sentence is the inspired

00:33:40.810 --> 00:33:45.300
mission to what degree do you feel
connected to something bigger?

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:49.670
Something bigger that your team is
enabling, your company is enabling, and

00:33:49.670 --> 00:33:53.700
you're feeling connected to the world
because as the meaning of happiness shifts

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:57.930
in these systematic ways and you move from
feeling excitement to wanting money and

00:33:57.930 --> 00:33:59.010
status.

00:33:59.010 --> 00:34:03.640
To wanting some element of balance,
you will need that desire for impact and

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:04.380
significance.

00:34:04.380 --> 00:34:10.142
I think Mystree's really done a remarkable
job if you dive into their website,

00:34:10.142 --> 00:34:13.238
and you understand their business model,

00:34:13.238 --> 00:34:17.638
they've done purpose in a really
clever and authentic way.

00:34:17.638 --> 00:34:23.187
I think Levo League has done it,
as well as Amour Vert.

00:34:23.187 --> 00:34:28.022
Lastly I wanted to mention I think stories
are important not just as an asset for

00:34:28.022 --> 00:34:30.670
companies and leaders, but also in life.

00:34:30.670 --> 00:34:33.710
So some of you guys know
that I have three kids.

00:34:35.510 --> 00:34:38.654
And they're now 14, 14, and 10.

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:44.280
And story as a scientist
trained to disregard story,

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:48.850
literally told that some us the story
you will not pay attention to it,

00:34:48.850 --> 00:34:52.920
certainly you want to make
a decision base on it.

00:34:52.920 --> 00:34:56.750
So, it's been really an actually
interesting last five years

00:34:56.750 --> 00:34:59.760
where story was shared immediate
fundamentally change my life because

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:03.510
I was wanted to be an oncologist.

00:35:03.510 --> 00:35:09.860
And, there was a time
where I would try and

00:35:09.860 --> 00:35:15.460
train in terms of medicine, and realized,
I'm not going to be the mom I want to be.

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:17.990
I'm going to end up being a professor.

00:35:17.990 --> 00:35:20.750
And, the reason I chose to
be a professor was because I

00:35:20.750 --> 00:35:22.360
really wanted to be this great mom.

00:35:22.360 --> 00:35:27.520
And also, a great wife and
a great friend, to my family.

00:35:27.520 --> 00:35:31.900
And so, that was the, really the impetus,
to be a marketing professor.

00:35:31.900 --> 00:35:34.250
But I have to tell you a secret.

00:35:34.250 --> 00:35:37.480
Just among the whatever,
300+ live stream of us.

00:35:37.480 --> 00:35:41.747
I don't even like marketing, so
I might not even like business.

00:35:41.747 --> 00:35:44.678
So I'm finding myself as
a marketing professor at

00:35:44.678 --> 00:35:48.527
a business school partly because my dad,
raise your hand, Dad.

00:35:48.527 --> 00:35:50.246
Yep.

00:35:50.246 --> 00:35:51.035
There you go.

00:35:51.035 --> 00:35:52.544
Say hi everyone.

00:35:52.544 --> 00:35:53.474
Hi everyone.

00:35:53.474 --> 00:36:00.060
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Partly because

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:05.340
my dad was such a phenomenal father and
I saw my father who's a professor

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:09.490
just be remarkable and
he raised us with stories,

00:36:09.490 --> 00:36:14.650
he would take us on special days where my
two sisters and I would get to go out with

00:36:14.650 --> 00:36:19.210
my father and he would take us, basically,
which where he wanted to go And

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:22.990
we would never want to go, but
he called it a special day.

00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:25.160
And so therefore we were so excited.

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:31.340
And when they would take pictures and
then we would remember it,

00:36:31.340 --> 00:36:36.470
and we would anticipate it as special,
we would remember it as special.

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:38.970
And even if we had a horrible
time at the cow game or

00:36:38.970 --> 00:36:43.500
whatever it was, we did not remember and
we did not anticipate to remember.

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:44.620
And that way of living.

00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:47.500
That way of raising us as kids

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:51.990
based on story became incredibly
incredibly important to me.

00:36:51.990 --> 00:36:56.680
Such that I made a career
decision to be a professor.

00:36:56.680 --> 00:37:02.450
And hopefully be that parent and
partner that I had seen in my family.

00:37:02.450 --> 00:37:06.390
And so
that was kind of the context of living.

00:37:06.390 --> 00:37:12.740
Story as a way of living certainly
infiltrated the way I was brought up,

00:37:12.740 --> 00:37:17.160
and it's impacted the way
we've raised our kids, and

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:19.780
I wanted to share two anecdotes with you.

00:37:20.930 --> 00:37:25.470
The first is the way,
I have to get into marriage therapy,

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:29.250
but the way my husband and
I actually relate

00:37:29.250 --> 00:37:34.670
It's fundamentally different
because of a story shared with me.

00:37:34.670 --> 00:37:37.000
To give you a little bit of
background on my husband,

00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:41.880
Andy Smith, who's a venture capitalist,
at the time, maybe five or

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:46.220
6 years ago or a little bit more,
he was running global brands at Dolby.

00:37:47.230 --> 00:37:55.180
And was really enamored with technology,
IOT, social media, etc.

00:37:55.180 --> 00:37:59.890
I thought social media was complete
crap and was so skeptical that

00:38:01.190 --> 00:38:05.350
we would really argue at the dinner table.

00:38:05.350 --> 00:38:06.320
Even in front of our kids.

00:38:06.320 --> 00:38:07.840
What did you do today?

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:09.870
I created a social media strategy for

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:12.050
Dole Band and
I'd say that seems like a waste of time.

00:38:12.050 --> 00:38:15.825
They're a business to business brand.

00:38:15.825 --> 00:38:19.280
And he'd say you're a marketing
professor at Standford and

00:38:19.280 --> 00:38:21.188
you've never been on Facebook.

00:38:21.188 --> 00:38:24.505
That doesn't seem smart.

00:38:24.505 --> 00:38:25.980
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:38:25.980 --> 00:38:29.935
whenever we disagree I usually
run a study to see if I'm right.

00:38:29.935 --> 00:38:31.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:38:31.030 --> 00:38:35.550
I asked a hundred people representative
of the United States what they thought of

00:38:35.550 --> 00:38:41.010
social media and about 50% of
them said very positive words and

00:38:41.010 --> 00:38:44.990
the other 50 or so percent of them
again said a lot of negative words,

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:47.850
which at least made me feel vindicated.

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:52.920
So you know in this belief
that social media was

00:38:52.920 --> 00:38:58.400
completely destroying
the world at that time, and

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:05.200
remember I'm heads down publishing
my papers, analysing big data sets,

00:39:07.210 --> 00:39:10.840
and sort of disagreeing
with Andy on a few things.

00:39:10.840 --> 00:39:13.700
But I was teaching this creativity and
innovation class,

00:39:13.700 --> 00:39:18.030
and one of my students,
Robert who some of you might know,

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:21.930
shared with me a story at the end of
the class of how the class impacted him.

00:39:21.930 --> 00:39:27.480
And he shared the story of Sameer, his
best friend here, a Stanford undergrad,

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:31.560
who went off and created his own company,
just like 20% of you.

00:39:31.560 --> 00:39:32.630
And got married.

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:37.201
Fell in love with Rena and
was diagnosed with leukemia

00:39:37.201 --> 00:39:41.885
while they were traveling in India and
he was shocked.

00:39:41.885 --> 00:39:46.638
Called Robert and Robert was shocked and
asked their friend network what they

00:39:46.638 --> 00:39:51.609
should be doing and they learned that
another friend of theirs, here and

00:39:51.609 --> 00:39:55.117
Fremont was also recently
diagnosed with leukemia, and

00:39:55.117 --> 00:39:59.531
many of you know this data, but
I will just share it with you in silence.

00:41:19.444 --> 00:41:23.348
So Robert wrote a story about Sameer and
that story about Sameer and

00:41:23.348 --> 00:41:25.789
those first two paragraphs was so clear,

00:41:25.789 --> 00:41:29.800
that if anyone got this email
they felt like they knew him.

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:31.630
You're so often asked to do things but

00:41:31.630 --> 00:41:35.130
without a story you're not
compelled to read or act.

00:41:35.130 --> 00:41:37.620
And then the clear call to action,
please get registered.

00:41:37.620 --> 00:41:39.750
Spread the word and learn more.

00:41:39.750 --> 00:41:44.260
Come to these websites and they took that
simple story with a call to action and

00:41:44.260 --> 00:41:48.940
they harnessed social media and
Robert reversed the rules.

00:41:48.940 --> 00:41:50.900
He did things in very unexpected ways.

00:41:50.900 --> 00:41:52.450
He didn't just build up the story and

00:41:52.450 --> 00:41:55.980
put it on a website and
share it via social media channels.

00:41:55.980 --> 00:42:00.300
They actually created two organizations,
team Vinay and Team Sameer and

00:42:00.300 --> 00:42:03.950
used Google Docs and
other tools to be able to communicate.

00:42:03.950 --> 00:42:07.670
These instant websites were
also clear calls to action,

00:42:07.670 --> 00:42:12.260
where if you double clicked any of
the links, up came an essentially

00:42:12.260 --> 00:42:17.350
a dragonfly type of model where any
of these links off of the website.

00:42:17.350 --> 00:42:19.200
So if you were here at Stanford and

00:42:19.200 --> 00:42:23.640
you wanted to run a corporate drive,
up would come a bone marrow drive to work,

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:26.180
a dummies guide and,
all of these links are live.

00:42:26.180 --> 00:42:30.600
So, if you double click the text in red,
up comes a letter.

00:42:30.600 --> 00:42:32.960
Dear Garth,
I want to run a bone marrow drive here,

00:42:32.960 --> 00:42:35.360
this Friday, on alumni weekend.

00:42:35.360 --> 00:42:39.410
This is what we need to do, cut, paste,
send and even a thank you letter.

00:42:39.410 --> 00:42:42.590
We ran a drive last Friday,
we got 200 people in.

00:42:42.590 --> 00:42:43.581
Thank you very much.

00:42:43.581 --> 00:42:46.007
This is what we need to do next cut,
paste, send.

00:42:46.007 --> 00:42:52.010
So that social change in a box, made that
story ignite in very significant ways.

00:42:52.010 --> 00:42:55.060
And many of you know, people acted.

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:57.720
They created Facebook ads,
and YouTube videos, and

00:42:57.720 --> 00:43:01.280
shared it on their own social
media channels and through that,

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:06.290
the result in 11 weeks was
470 bone marrow drives run.

00:43:06.290 --> 00:43:10.230
Nearly 25,000 South Asians registered,
and in that time,

00:43:10.230 --> 00:43:16.360
Sameer found a perfect match,
and Vinay found a good match.

00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:20.890
Sameer now shared his story, even though
he was sort of private about this all

00:43:20.890 --> 00:43:24.150
because he wanted to raise venture
money and he did not want this out.

00:43:24.150 --> 00:43:26.753
And he wanted it to be private but

00:43:26.753 --> 00:43:31.510
once 25,000 people acted for
him, he wanted open up.

00:43:31.510 --> 00:43:32.480
He started blogging.

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:34.060
He shared that story.

00:43:34.060 --> 00:43:37.400
And he started appreciating
life in a very meaningful way.

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:41.990
Not in a happy, excited way,
but in a deeply meaningful way.

00:43:41.990 --> 00:43:46.810
In a way that talked a lot about
significance a lot about savoring a lot

00:43:46.810 --> 00:43:50.540
about others and appreciation and
it wasn't winning, winning, winning.

00:43:50.540 --> 00:43:55.050
It was hard, it was winning and
losing and being scared, and

00:43:55.050 --> 00:43:58.600
wanting something and feeling grateful for
what others had done.

00:43:59.700 --> 00:44:02.290
He even put his bone marrow
registry onto YouTube so

00:44:02.290 --> 00:44:06.070
that in future years people could
see that actually giving and

00:44:06.070 --> 00:44:09.460
receiving bone marrow is not
nearly as difficult as you think.

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:14.758
And the lessons they learned was
that power of a single focused goal,

00:44:14.758 --> 00:44:17.830
20,000 south Asians in the registry.

00:44:17.830 --> 00:44:21.910
The power of reversing the rules,
doing things in very unexpected ways, and

00:44:21.910 --> 00:44:22.870
grabbing attention.

00:44:22.870 --> 00:44:24.990
And how do you tell a good and
truthful story,

00:44:24.990 --> 00:44:28.170
because otherwise people simply won't act.

00:44:28.170 --> 00:44:32.020
And then, how do you design for
the network, allowing everyone to,

00:44:32.020 --> 00:44:35.110
in fact, contribute and
choose their own weapon.

00:44:36.570 --> 00:44:39.110
Sameer, though, relapsed within
three months of of his transplant,

00:44:39.110 --> 00:44:43.400
at the end of my class and
that was him two days before with Reena.

00:44:45.270 --> 00:44:48.660
We celebrated his life by sharing
a memorial service in the world, so

00:44:48.660 --> 00:44:53.050
a global wide webcast with
6,000 people participating.

00:44:53.050 --> 00:44:55.690
Vinay also had a successful transplant but

00:44:55.690 --> 00:45:01.500
he also relapsed a few months after
Sameer and he passed on in June.

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:06.300
But the purpose revealed,
as 40% of you here know,

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:11.210
is that 266 people were
matched in one year alone.

00:45:11.210 --> 00:45:14.710
And that is 266 people that don't
just have the gift of hope, but

00:45:14.710 --> 00:45:19.380
the gift of time, because odds of survival
go up from 40% to 80% if you find

00:45:19.380 --> 00:45:21.110
a perfect match immediately.

00:45:23.300 --> 00:45:27.080
And the final thoughts is that most
revolutions are sparked by the actions of

00:45:27.080 --> 00:45:29.870
the few, and
your biggest asset is a clear mind and

00:45:29.870 --> 00:45:32.549
a large idea to find that
ignition point and light it.

00:45:34.100 --> 00:45:39.420
And as I was listening to Robert's story,
Robert, just like you, my students sitting

00:45:39.420 --> 00:45:46.710
in a seat just like yours sharing
a story that just made me sob.

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:50.960
All I could think about
is what would I do now,

00:45:50.960 --> 00:45:55.320
could I water the story, would the story
grow, how would that make me feel and

00:45:55.320 --> 00:45:58.550
all I could think about was
that would make me happy.

00:45:58.550 --> 00:46:03.570
So I talked to Andy that night and
I said, what could we do to help?

00:46:03.570 --> 00:46:06.616
I admitted that social media
had some redeeming feature.

00:46:06.616 --> 00:46:09.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And we started interviewing people,

00:46:09.970 --> 00:46:13.200
people that harness social networks,
harness story.

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:17.450
To significant in fact, and we found
that all of them did these four things.

00:46:17.450 --> 00:46:21.860
All of them had a single focus goal,
not two, not three, but a single one.

00:46:21.860 --> 00:46:23.280
They all grabbed attention and

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:26.280
reversed the rules,
did things in very unexpected ways.

00:46:26.280 --> 00:46:28.610
They all engaged and
were anchored on audience,

00:46:28.610 --> 00:46:30.990
creating authentic, relevant content.

00:46:30.990 --> 00:46:34.120
And they all enabled action,
this idea design for

00:46:34.120 --> 00:46:39.360
the network versus an individual
defined all of their movements.

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:43.940
So that story well-told
created 470 bone marrow drives

00:46:43.940 --> 00:46:46.340
all across the United States.

00:46:46.340 --> 00:46:51.160
And then,
it lead us to basically right this book,

00:46:51.160 --> 00:46:55.460
The Dragonfly Effect,
which I didn't ever want to write.

00:46:55.460 --> 00:46:59.870
But it just kind of, I felt like
Sameer was writing it for us and

00:46:59.870 --> 00:47:03.350
then, it allowed,
I used to work with 100,000 sheiks.

00:47:03.350 --> 00:47:08.231
These 12 students here at Stanford who,
in honor and legacy of Sameer and Vinay,

00:47:08.231 --> 00:47:11.859
got over 113,000 people in
the bone marrow registry,

00:47:11.859 --> 00:47:15.228
in one year alone harnessing
social networks and story.

00:47:15.228 --> 00:47:20.796
And that led to OpenIDEO participating and
co-creating a design challenge

00:47:20.796 --> 00:47:25.587
to electrify and ignite over
a million people internationally.

00:47:25.587 --> 00:47:31.043
Which led to at least one match of
Amit Gupta with his friends and family and

00:47:31.043 --> 00:47:36.430
network who is alive and healthy
today when diagnosed with leukemia.

00:47:36.430 --> 00:47:39.220
We found a match and that makes me happy.

00:47:39.220 --> 00:47:42.629
And it makes me happy
in a way that's very,

00:47:42.629 --> 00:47:48.225
very anchored on connectedness and
gratefulness and blessedness and

00:47:48.225 --> 00:47:54.699
I think all of us have closet careers and
all of us like maybe being an oncologist.

00:47:54.699 --> 00:47:59.875
And this opportunity for you to be
back here at your reunion in the space

00:47:59.875 --> 00:48:05.506
of potentially thinking of that six
word story that defined your time here.

00:48:05.506 --> 00:48:08.101
And then,
thinking about your next chapter,

00:48:08.101 --> 00:48:10.494
what do you want that
six word story to be and

00:48:10.494 --> 00:48:14.950
to what degree do you want to have a very
different definition of happiness?

00:48:14.950 --> 00:48:18.638
To what degree do you want to search or
chase different things and

00:48:18.638 --> 00:48:20.498
might be create legacy for you?

00:48:21.704 --> 00:48:23.410
So, what to do on Monday?

00:48:23.410 --> 00:48:28.300
Three things, I hope you continue to think
of story as an asset for your company and

00:48:28.300 --> 00:48:31.440
design with authenticity,
empathy and purpose.

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:34.660
I hope you've continue to
think of story as life.

00:48:34.660 --> 00:48:39.660
With our three kids we now ask over
the dinner table what was the story of

00:48:39.660 --> 00:48:46.400
the day because it turns out when you ask
what did you do today, you get nothing.

00:48:46.400 --> 00:48:50.210
But when you ask them to share just one
story, it can be funny, it can be sad,

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:51.660
it can be anything.

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:55.670
They will lead to conversations
that are much richer, and

00:48:55.670 --> 00:48:59.940
your relationship will be much
richer at least, it was with ours.

00:48:59.940 --> 00:49:02.130
Stories can clarify our past and

00:49:02.130 --> 00:49:07.650
they can guide our future stories
create and capture meaning.

00:49:07.650 --> 00:49:13.280
So bank your stories just like you
did with me, in 30 seconds and

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:17.310
you'll find that the most important
stories that define your past and

00:49:17.310 --> 00:49:21.906
will indicate your future are those
that are indeed personal, distinct,

00:49:21.906 --> 00:49:28.030
relevant, memorable they
depict something larger.

00:49:28.030 --> 00:49:34.650
They create legacy, so in conclusion,
tell me the facts and I'll learn.

00:49:34.650 --> 00:49:37.580
Tell me the truth and
I'll believe but tell me a story and

00:49:37.580 --> 00:49:40.497
it will live in my heart forever.

00:49:40.497 --> 00:49:42.337
Thank you.

00:49:42.337 --> 00:49:46.145
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:46.145 --> 00:49:50.649
[MUSIC]

